On many tanker and LPG voyages, port time costs more than any argument about the freight rate. Laytime and demurrage decide who pays for that time. Most disputes are not about the law: they come from loose wording in the fixture and missing times in the port documents. This guide covers the points we check on every voyage charter.

## The terms

- Laycan: the window in which the ship must arrive and tender notice of readiness. If the ship misses the cancelling date, the charterer may cancel.
- Laytime: the time the charterer may use to load and discharge without paying extra. Tanker charters usually give a total in running hours for loading and discharging together; LPG charters may set it per port or per operation.
- Notice of readiness (NOR): the master's notice that the ship has arrived and is ready to load or discharge. A valid NOR is what starts the clock.
- Demurrage: an agreed daily rate, paid pro rata, for time used beyond laytime. It is liquidated damages, so the rate is not re-argued later.
- Detention: damages for delay that is not covered by the demurrage clause, for example before laytime could start.

## When the clock starts

Under common tanker forms such as ASBATANKVOY, laytime starts a fixed number of hours (often six) after a valid NOR, or when the ship is all fast at berth, whichever comes first. The notice must be given at the right place and at a time the charter party allows, and the ship must really be ready. An invalid NOR is the most common reason a demurrage claim fails, so we confirm the arrival location and NOR rules before the ship arrives.

## What stops the clock

- Shifting from the anchorage to the berth, usually excluded even when the ship is already on demurrage.
- Delays caused by the ship: breakdowns, crew problems, or failing to discharge at the warranted pumping rate or pressure.
- Time spent on the owners' own business, such as bunkering or tank cleaning, unless done in the charterer's time.
- Events named in the charter party. Some forms pay only half the demurrage rate for delays from weather, fire, explosion or strikes.
- For gas carriers: cooling down, gassing up and purging. Check whether this time counts, and who pays the cargo or nitrogen used.

## A worked example

{% routeTable
   caption="Illustrative laytime calculation, round numbers only" %}
{% table %}
- Item
- Hours
---
- Laytime allowed (load + discharge, running hours)
- 72
---
- Time counted at load port
- 40
---
- Time counted at discharge port, after exceptions
- 56
---
- Total time counted
- 96
---
- Time on demurrage (96 − 72)
- 24 = 1.0 day
{% /table %}
{% /routeTable %}

If the agreed demurrage rate is X dollars per day, the claim is 1.0 × X, pro rata for part days. In practice the calculation also tracks NOR times, hoses connected and disconnected, documents on board and every interruption, port by port.

## Documents that decide claims

1. Statement of facts, signed by the terminal or agent, with every time stamp.
1. Notice of readiness and the time it was accepted.
1. Pumping logs and manifold pressure records for any claim that the ship pumped slowly.
1. Letters of protest for delays, short loading or disputed times.
1. The claim itself, submitted with all supporting documents before the time bar. Many tanker forms set 90 days, some fewer; a claim sent late, or without the papers, can be lost completely.

